this file.

WHAT THE CHECKER GOT RIGHT
- The sparkline exists on the Budget card and is coloured by bare sign:
  web/templates/components/kpis.html:189-192 emits `#sparkline-budget` with
  `data-values="{{toJSON .Metrics.CombinedCumulativeBalance}}" data-mode="balance"`, and
  web/static/js/charts.js:281-282 does `values.map(v => v > 0 ? v : 0)` /
  `v < 0 ? v : 0` with no dead band and no neutral branch. Accurate.
- The attempt's own fix list is complete and correct. The contested verdict concedes this
  ("tint/headline/bucket-row/banner cannot disagree anymore"), and both other lanes
  independently confirm it (W4.4.checker-tests, W4.4.checker-a11y).

WHERE THE CHECKER IS FACTUALLY WRONG — THE "SAME FIGURE" PREMISE FAILS
The FAIL rests on one load-bearing claim: that the sparkline's last point IS the figure
BudgetVerdict classifies (balance-signed), so a dead-band delta produces "On budget" next
to a red last segment. That is not what the code computes.

  balanceTail = combinedTarget * len(trendMonths) - totalSpend      (metrics.go:308-311)
  delta       = totalSpend - combinedTarget * MonthsBetween(start,end)  (metrics.go:250)
  => balanceTail = -delta + combinedTarget * (len(trendMonths) - MonthsInRange)

MonthsBetween (metrics.go:25-31) is days/30.44 — never an integer for a calendar range —
while the balance series steps in whole transaction-months, and is truncated to the LAST
SIX months (metrics.go:277-279). The offset term is therefore systematically nonzero and
scales with the target. Measured, at a live-scale combined target (living 8124.60 +
healthcare 575), spend tuned so delta = +$0.50 (inside the dead band):

  one 31-day month (Jan)      MonthsInRange=1.0185  delta=+0.50  balanceTail= -161.27  RED
  three months Jan-Mar (90d)  MonthsInRange=2.9569  delta=+0.50  balanceTail= +374.64  GREEN
  one 28-day month (Feb)      MonthsInRange=0.9199  delta=+0.50  balanceTail= +696.18  GREEN

The verdict's worked example — "below[last] = -0.50 < 0" — does not occur. And the
disagreement is not a dead-band phenomenon at all. Sweeping delta on the Jan-Mar range:

  delta   +0.00  card "On budget"      balanceTail  +375.14  sparkline GREEN
  delta +100.00  card "$100.00 over"   balanceTail  +275.14  sparkline GREEN
  delta +300.00  card "$300.00 over"   balanceTail   +75.14  sparkline GREEN
  delta +374.00  card "$374.00 over"   balanceTail    +1.14  sparkline GREEN
  delta +376.00  card "$376.00 over"   balanceTail    -0.86  sparkline RED

The sparkline's zero crossing sits at delta ~= +$375 here, not at 0 and not at +/-$1. The
project's own cited test is the evidence AGAINST the checker, not for it:
internal/services/metrics/metrics_test.go:942-951 allows $100 of slack at a $1,550 target
precisely because the two quantities are computed on different bases; that slack scales to
hundreds of dollars at live targets. On the run spec's own live case (7.8 months) the
6-month truncation makes the series a different aggregate outright.

CONSEQUENCE FOR RULING 2026-08-29a
29a's predicate is "every surface that renders the SAME figure". The sparkline renders a
different aggregate (whole-month basis, <=6-month window, per-month running series), and
applies no W4 threshold at all — a chart baseline at zero, not the $1 dead band. The
predicate is not met. Worse, the remedy the FAIL implies is wrong on the merits: piping
BudgetVerdict.IsOver/IsUnder into renderSparkline would NOT reconcile the two, because the
gap is a basis mismatch, not a missing dead band. Accepting this FAIL would send the
worker to apply a fix that does not fix the thing.

